      qs: [
        ["A patient says, 'I know I should exercise, but I'm not sure I can fit it in.' Which stage of change is MOST likely?", ["Contemplation", "Precontemplation", "Preparation", "Maintenance"], "She recognizes the need but is ambivalent, the hallmark of contemplation. Precontemplation would deny the need, and preparation involves a concrete plan."],
        ["A patient has walked 30 minutes, five days a week, for the past three months. Which stage best describes him?", ["Action", "Maintenance", "Preparation", "Contemplation"], "The behavior has been in place for less than six months, so he is in action. Maintenance begins after about six months."],
        ["For a patient in precontemplation, what is the MOST appropriate PT strategy?", ["Raise awareness and explore what matters to him", "Give a detailed 8-week exercise program today", "Set a SMART goal for daily running this week", "Focus on relapse prevention and coping strategies"], "People in precontemplation are not ready to act, so the goal is awareness and connection to values. Detailed programs suit later stages."],
        ["Which goal BEST meets SMART criteria?", ["Walk 20 minutes after lunch, 3 days a week, for 4 weeks", "Become more active and lose weight this year", "Exercise as much as possible whenever I can", "Get stronger so my back stops hurting"], "Only the first is specific, measurable, achievable, and time-bound. The others are vague and hard to track."],
        ["Which statement is an example of an open question in motivational interviewing?", ["What would being more active make possible for you?", "Did you manage to do all your exercises this week?", "Do you want to stay healthy as you get older?", "Can you walk for 20 minutes without stopping?"], "Open questions invite elaboration. The others can be answered with yes or no."],
        ["A patient says, 'I want to get back to hiking with my daughter.' This statement is BEST described as:", ["Change talk", "Sustain talk", "Discord", "A righting reflex"], "Expressing desire and reasons for change is change talk, which the clinician should reinforce."],
        ["A patient explains why he cannot exercise, and the PT responds with a list of counterarguments. This is an example of:", ["The righting reflex", "An affirmation", "Evocation", "A complex reflection"], "Arguing for change is the righting reflex, which usually triggers more sustain talk. MI favors reflecting and exploring instead."],
        ["Which statement is an MI affirmation?", ["You came in today even though work was hectic", "You need to try harder with the exercises", "Why haven't you been walking this week?", "Most people your age exercise more than that"], "Affirmations recognize the person's strengths and efforts. The others are directive, judgmental, or comparative."],
        ["On an importance ruler a patient rates exercise a 4 out of 10. What is the BEST follow-up question?", ["Why a 4 and not a 1?", "Why isn't it a 10?", "Shouldn't it be higher?", "What stops you from caring?"], "Asking why the score is not lower draws out the patient's own reasons for change. Asking why not higher invites sustain talk."],
        ["Before suggesting a walking program, an MI-consistent PT would MOST likely say:", ["Would it be okay if I shared some ideas?", "Here's exactly what you need to do.", "You have to start walking today.", "I'll tell you what works best."], "Asking permission respects autonomy and makes the patient more open to advice."],
        ["Which element of MI spirit refers to drawing out the patient's own reasons for change?", ["Evocation", "Acceptance", "Compassion", "Partnership"], "Evocation means eliciting the person's own motivation rather than imposing the clinician's reasons."],
        ["A patient struggles to remember balance exercises. Which strategy BEST supports habit formation?", ["Link them to brushing his teeth each morning", "Do them whenever he happens to remember", "Add more exercises to make the session worthwhile", "Do them only when he feels unsteady"], "Habit stacking provides a reliable cue. Relying on memory or symptoms is inconsistent."],
        ["A patient who has exercised for two months misses a week and says she has 'ruined everything'. What is the BEST response?", ["Relapse is common; restart with a small goal", "She must start over from the very beginning", "Missing a week shows she lacks motivation", "She should double her exercise to catch up"], "Lapses are a normal part of change, and a missed period does not erase progress. Restarting small rebuilds confidence."],
        ["How long does it usually take to form a new habit?", ["It varies widely, often weeks to months", "Exactly 21 days for most people", "About 3 days of consistent practice", "At least 2 years for any behavior"], "Habit formation time differs by person and behavior. The '21 days' figure is a myth without strong support."],
        ["A patient says, 'Exercise is important to me (9/10), but I don't think I can do it (3/10).' What should the PT focus on FIRST?", ["Building confidence with a small, achievable step", "Convincing her that exercise is important", "Giving her a longer, harder program", "Discharging her until she feels ready"], "Importance is already high, so confidence is the barrier. Small successes build self-efficacy."]
      ]
