        ["Which action is within a PT's scope regarding nutrition?", ["Sharing general Dietary Guidelines information", "Writing a medical diet plan for kidney disease", "Prescribing specific supplements for diabetes", "Designing an individualized calorie-restricted diet"], "General information is within PT scope. Individualized medical nutrition therapy and supplement prescriptions belong to dietitians and physicians."],
        ["Which nutrients are MOST important to support wound healing?", ["Adequate calories, protein, vitamin C, and zinc", "Caffeine, sodium, and saturated fat in larger amounts", "Alcohol and simple sugars for quick energy", "Fiber alone in very large daily amounts"], "Healing needs energy, protein for collagen and immune function, vitamin C for collagen synthesis, and zinc for cell growth."],
        ["A patient's urine is consistently dark amber and she reports dizziness on standing. This MOST likely suggests:", ["Inadequate hydration", "Excess protein intake", "Normal hydration status", "Low carbohydrate intake"], "Dark urine and orthostatic dizziness are common signs of dehydration. Pale yellow urine suggests adequate hydration."],
        ["What does 'anabolic resistance' mean in older adults?", ["Muscle responds less to protein and exercise", "Muscle grows faster than in young adults", "The body stops absorbing carbohydrate", "Protein is stored as fat instead of muscle"], "Older muscle has a blunted protein synthesis response, which is why higher protein intake and resistance training are recommended."],
        ["A teenage athlete tells the PT she skips meals to stay light and has missed several periods. What is the MOST appropriate action?", ["Refer to her physician and a dietitian promptly", "Tell her to eat more and continue training", "Reduce her training load and recheck in a month", "Recommend an over-the-counter multivitamin"], "Restricted intake with menstrual dysfunction suggests low energy availability and possible disordered eating, which needs medical and dietitian care."],
        ["About what percentage of adult daily calories should come from fat according to the AMDR?", ["About 20-35%", "About 5-10%", "About 45-65%", "About 60-75%"], "The AMDR for fat is roughly 20-35% of calories. 45-65% is the carbohydrate range."],
        ["During PT, a patient asks whether he should take a high-dose supplement he saw online for joint pain. The BEST response is:", ["Discuss it with his physician or a dietitian", "Recommend it if it is sold without prescription", "Tell him all supplements are dangerous", "Suggest doubling the dose for faster results"], "Supplements can interact with medications and conditions. Specific supplement advice should come from the physician, pharmacist, or dietitian."]
